window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-64fb6be95001e945"]={"key":"ny_b1-64fb6be95001e945","group":"ny_b1","uid":"64fb6be95001e945","rank":88,"title":"tarnbeck inbound supply configuration proposal","occupation":"buyers_purchasing_agents","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Joaquin Kirchner, the senior buyer for Tarnbeck Hydraulics, and Lieselotte Bruhn needs a defensible inbound supply configuration before the annual cast-manifold contract round closes in ten business days. Tarnbeck is moving the manifold supply from an air-freighted Asian source to a sea-freight footing; Vantridge Works and Goldfinch Foundry are the commercial parties whose positions must be addressed for Legal counsel and the contracting team. Prepare `inbound_supply_configuration_proposal.docx` and put it at the top level of the working directory, using exactly that filename. The proposal must make one clear recommendation covering the Incoterm, order quantity, stocking point, and safety-stock level, then show the assumptions, projected inventory and cash effects, service or lead-time exposure, and the reasons credible alternatives were set aside. Use these planning facts without inventing missing commercial rates: annual demand is 2,400 manifolds, sea transit is normally 35 days with possible additional delay of 10 days, air transit is 7 days, a full-container purchase is 600 units, the existing dock can hold no more than 400 units awaiting put-away, the treasury cap for purchase-value inventory committed to this program is $180,000, and the current planning value is $240 per manifold. The proposal must distinguish purchase value from freight, duty, brokerage, storage, and other landed-cost elements, because the should-cost model needs freight visibility. Address the commercial and contractual implications for both United States and German operations under Incoterms 2020, but do not present the document as legal advice or invent duty rates, freight quotes, insurance coverage, or supplier promises. The familiar assumption that full-container quantities are automatically the economical answer is under pressure here, and there is no time for a multi-month pilot or capital work to enlarge the dock, so explain what professional fallback is defensible. Keep the document to no more than three pages, write for a counsel or contracting stakeholder who will not ask you to decode purchasing shorthand, and end with the decision itself rather than a list of future actions.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `inbound_supply_configuration_proposal.docx` — A concise doctrinal decision memorandum recommending one inbound cast-manifold supply configuration and documenting its operational, financial, Incoterms, and contractual rationale. (Must be saved at the top level of the solver's working directory with exactly the specified filename.; Must not exceed three pages.; Must contain exactly one recommended configuration and a clear final decision.; Must compare credible alternatives and distinguish facts, assumptions, calculations, and unresolved commercial inputs.; Must address United States and German operations under Incoterms 2020 without presenting unsupported legal conclusions.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/inbound_supply_configuration_proposal.docx","bytes":41006,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/c1a5c5b49ad6864fbeff796f7b66635d6fd7a967d2588651cca1a487db7d4b5e.docx","sha256":"c1a5c5b49ad6864fbeff796f7b66635d6fd7a967d2588651cca1a487db7d4b5e","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"TARNBECK HYDRAULICS\nINBOUND SUPPLY CONFIGURATION PROPOSAL — CAST MANIFOLD\nTo\nLieselotte Bruhn; Legal Counsel & Contracting Stakeholders\nFrom\nJoaquin Kirchner, Senior Buyer\nDate\n20 August 2026\nDecision window\nAnnual contract round closes in 10 business days\nDecision requested. Authorize one bounded sea-freight configuration for Vantridge Works and Goldfinch Foundry, subject to the commercial confirmations below. This is procurement planning, not legal advice.\nRECOMMENDED CONFIGURATION (ONE CHOICE)\nIncoterm: FCA Vantridge Works export handover point, supplier site — Incoterms® 2020 (exact site address in the purchase order). Order quantity: 300 cast manifolds per ocean replenishment. Stocking point: Tarnbeck Regional 3PL Stock Point (near the plant); safety stock remains there. Safety stock: 120 manifolds at the 3PL, released only against a plant requirement.\nWhy this configuration fits the stated boundary\nDemand basis. Annual demand is 2,400 manifolds = 200 per 30-day month (about 6.67/day). Normal sea transit is 35 days; the possible additional delay is 10 days, so the exposed planning window is up to 45 days. Air is 7 days and is retained only as an emergency contingency; its rate and availability are not supplied.\nInventory and cash test. The 300-unit order-cycle quantity is distinct from the 120-unit safety stock. If both are committed purchase-value inventory, exposure is (300 + 120) × $240 = $100,800, below Treasury’s $180,000 cap by $79,200. The 300-unit ocean receipt is below the existing dock’s 400-unit maximum; a 600-unit full-container receipt would exceed it by 200 units.\nService and operating control. The 120-unit buffer is approximately 18 days of average demand (120 ÷ 6.67), a bounded response to the 35-day sea lead time plus the possible 10-day delay—not a statistically optimal service claim. The 3PL receives the ocean lot, reports on-hand and aged units daily, and releases only scheduled quantities against a Tarnbeck production signal. A dock appointment and split-release instruction prevent an unplanned surge at the plant. Review the 120-unit assumption if two consecutive receipts exceed 45 days, or if rolling 90-day demand moves more than 15% from 2,400 annualized.\nCommercial and Incoterms® 2020 frame\nFCA is a delivery rule set, not a freight abbreviation. At the named Vantridge Works export handover point, Vantridge makes the goods available/handles the agreed handover; Tarnbeck (or its nominated carrier) arranges main carriage. Risk transfers at the FCA delivery event, while title/ownership, payment timing, and acceptance remain separate contract terms. Insurance is not presumed by FCA and must be separately confirmed. Freight, brokerage, duty, storage and other landed-cost elements remain visible as separate should-cost fields; no rate is invented here.\nUnited States and Germany. The US and German operating entities may have different importer-of-record, customs, tax, product-regulatory and recordkeeping duties. Incoterms® 2020 does not decide tariff classification, duty/tax rates, governing law, title or every customs obligation. Counsel must confirm contracting entities, governing law, named place, enforceability and importer/customs arrangement for each flow. This memo assumes none of those facts.\nAlternatives tested and bounded\nAlternative\nWhy credible\nWhy not base decision\n600-unit full-container to plant\nCommon “full-container is cheapest” rule of thumb; 600 × $240 = $144,000 purchase value.\nExceeds the 400-unit dock by 200; with 120 safety stock, $172,800 leaves little cap room for unquoted freight/duty/storage. Density savings can be lost to capacity and total landed cost.\n300 units under DDP\nSupplier-managed carriage/import can simplify handoff.\nDDP can bundle freight, duty and brokerage, reducing freight visibility; it does not settle title, payment, governing law or importer duties for US/Germany. No DDP quote or supplier commitment exists.\nConsignment stock near plant\nMay defer payment until withdrawal.\nOwnership, control, insurance, shrinkage, count rights and payment triggers require a bespoke agreement; consignment does not automatically satisfy Treasury control or prove capacity.\nSmaller ocean lots to plant only\nCould reduce on-site quantity.\nWithout the 3PL, the dock gates every receipt and gives no controlled home for the 120-unit buffer; extra handling and order frequency are unquoted.\nAir as normal mode\nSeven-day transit reduces delay exposure.\nAir is contingency, not the sea-freight footing requested; no air rate, capacity, emissions or landed-cost evidence is available.\nBounded fallback and contract controls\nNo multi-month pilot is available before the round closes, and dock enlargement/capital work is not an immediate remedy. Use a controlled first 300-unit ocean release under the same FCA/3PL configuration: pre-shipment document review; count/condition evidence at the 3PL; quarantine-and-claim process for shortage or nonconformance; and a release gate requiring production demand plus inventory reconciliation. This bounded release tests execution without changing the approved base configuration or claiming that long-run economics are validated.\nContract drafting boundary. Vantridge Works and Goldfinch Foundry instruments should identify the FCA named place, carrier nomination, delivery evidence, delay/damage/shortage and nonconformance notice path, and cost allocation after the FCA event. Payment terms, title/ownership, acceptance, insurance scope, customs/importer role and remedies must be stated separately. Freight, duty, brokerage, storage, insurance, supplier reliability and service history are unresolved commercial inputs; no unsupported supplier promise, quote, duty rate or legal conclusion is treated as fact. If verified freight/storage sensitivity makes 300 units uneconomic, that is a new approval—not an automatic move to 600 units, DDP, consignment or air.\nFINAL DECISION — Tarnbeck Hydraulics should contract for 300 cast manifolds per ocean replenishment on FCA Vantridge Works export handover point (Incoterms® 2020), hold the 120-manifold safety stock at the regional 3PL near the plant, and release stock to the plant against controlled production demand. This is the single approved inbound configuration for the ten-business-day contract round.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is a readable DOCX file named exactly inbound_supply_configuration_proposal.docx.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Filename and successful DOCX opening.","grader_hint":"Inspect the working-directory filename and open the file with a standard offline DOCX reader."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered memorandum is no more than three pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX to PDF or images and count pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document names Tarnbeck Hydraulics and identifies the cast manifold as the subject.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Company and product references in the document.","grader_hint":"Search the rendered text."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies Lieselotte Bruhn as the decision sponsor or recipient context and addresses counsel or contracting stakeholders.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Named audience and sponsor.","grader_hint":"Search the heading, opening, or audience statement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document states exactly one recommended inbound configuration rather than presenting several co-equal choices.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation statement.","grader_hint":"A preferred configuration must be unambiguous across the executive summary, analysis, and conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation explicitly states an Incoterm and version or otherwise clearly anchors the term to Incoterms 2020.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommended trade term.","grader_hint":"Look for a named Incoterm such as FCA, FOB, CIF, or DDP and Incoterms 2020."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation explicitly states one order quantity.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Configuration summary.","grader_hint":"The quantity must be a concrete number or clearly bounded quantity, not merely 'smaller batches.'"},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation explicitly names one stocking point and explains its relationship to the plant dock.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Stocking-point decision.","grader_hint":"Accept plant, an external 3PL, or another specific point only if its dock-capacity implications are explained."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation explicitly states one safety-stock level in units or an equivalent calculable quantity.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Safety-stock decision.","grader_hint":"A qualitative buffer alone does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum ends on the decision, not on a generic list of next steps.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraph or final decision box.","grader_hint":"The closing should reaffirm the selected configuration and authorization rather than defer the choice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document uses annual demand of 2,400 manifolds and shows its conversion to an average-period demand basis.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Demand calculation.","grader_hint":"Accept monthly or daily conversion if the period is stated and arithmetically consistent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document recognizes that a 600-unit full-container quantity is above the existing dock's 400-unit holding capacity.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dock constraint analysis.","grader_hint":"Check the comparison, not merely separate mentions of 600 and 400."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document calculates or bounds the purchase-value exposure of the selected order quantity at the stated $240 per manifold.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Order-value calculation.","grader_hint":"The calculation must use the chosen order quantity and identify it as purchase value."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document tests the selected configuration against the $180,000 purchase-value inventory cap rather than treating the cap as an unexamined fact.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cash-cap test.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit comparison, including how safety stock is treated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document distinguishes order-cycle quantity from safety stock and does not silently add or omit the safety stock when discussing committed inventory.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inventory model definitions.","grader_hint":"Check the arithmetic and the stated inventory interpretation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document incorporates the 35-day normal sea transit and the possible additional 10-day delay in its service or buffer reasoning.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Lead-time analysis.","grader_hint":"Both figures must affect the reasoning, not appear as disconnected background."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document distinguishes the seven-day air transit from sea transit and explains why air is not simply treated as the same operating mode.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Mode comparison.","grader_hint":"Accept a statement that air is an emergency or contingency mode if its cost and applicability are not invented."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The selected quantity, stocking point, and safety stock form a coherent response to both lead-time exposure and dock capacity.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Integrated configuration rationale.","grader_hint":"Do not award if one recommendation solves lead time only by contradicting the stated dock limit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the full-container-is-cheapest assumption as a rule of thumb or common assumption.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rule-of-thumb discussion.","grader_hint":"Equivalent wording is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document states when or why the full-container rule of thumb stops applying in this case, specifically linking it to the dock, cash, service, or total landed-cost boundary.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception analysis.","grader_hint":"A generic warning that rules of thumb have exceptions is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document recognizes that no multi-month pilot is available before the contract round.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Time constraint.","grader_hint":"Search for the ten-business-day or no-pilot constraint."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document proposes a defensible bounded fallback for the unavailable textbook pilot, without pretending that uncertainty has been eliminated.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Fallback control.","grader_hint":"Accept a staged, limited, conditional, or otherwise controlled approach if it is concrete and compatible with the selected configuration."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document addresses that dock enlargement or capital work is not available as the immediate solution.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Resource constraint treatment.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit exclusion of dock expansion as an immediate remedy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum explains why its recommendation is preferable to at least three credible alternative configurations.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternatives section or comparison table.","grader_hint":"Alternatives must differ materially in quantity, term, stocking point, safety stock, or consignment treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternatives analysis includes a full-container quantity as a rejected or bounded alternative.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternative comparison.","grader_hint":"Check for the 600-unit option or equivalent full-container option."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternatives analysis addresses DDP as an alternative or risk allocation issue.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incoterm comparison.","grader_hint":"A named DDP discussion is required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternatives analysis addresses consignment stock as an alternative or payment/cash-flow issue.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Stock ownership comparison.","grader_hint":"A named consignment discussion is required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document explains why consignment does not automatically satisfy the payment-terms or control objective.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Consignment analysis.","grader_hint":"Look for ownership, payment timing, control, accounting, or contract dependency rather than a bare rejection."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document treats stocking-point selection as an operational and financial choice rather than a neutral warehouse detail.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Stocking-point rationale.","grader_hint":"The selected point must be connected to dock flow, handling, visibility, or inventory exposure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states whether safety stock is held at the plant or another stocking point and keeps that location consistent with the recommendation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Configuration consistency.","grader_hint":"Cross-check every occurrence of safety-stock location."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The safety-stock rationale relates demand, sea lead time, delay exposure, and the cost or capacity of excess inventory.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Safety-stock reasoning.","grader_hint":"A number with no tradeoff explanation does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states what event or evidence would trigger review or adjustment of the safety-stock assumption.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review trigger.","grader_hint":"Accept a measurable demand, delay, service, or inventory signal."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document names Incoterms 2020 and treats the selected term as a rule set for delivery, cost, and risk allocation rather than as a freight abbreviation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incoterms framework.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit treatment of obligations, costs, and risk."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document distinguishes transfer of risk from transfer of title or ownership.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Contractual distinction.","grader_hint":"Award only if the distinction is explicit and not implied solely by an Incoterm name."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document identifies who is responsible for arranging main carriage under the recommended Incoterm.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Carrier responsibility statement.","grader_hint":"The stated responsibility must be consistent with the chosen Incoterm."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document identifies the delivery point or named place required to make the selected Incoterm operational.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Named-place treatment.","grader_hint":"A term without a usable named place is incomplete."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document explains how freight-rate visibility is preserved or lost under the selected term and under DDP.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Should-cost treatment.","grader_hint":"The comparison must address transparency of freight or landed-cost components."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document explains the insurance implication of the chosen Incoterm or states that insurance coverage must be separately confirmed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Insurance treatment.","grader_hint":"Do not require a specific coverage level; require a correctly bounded statement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document states that customs, import, duty, or tax treatment depends on the applicable jurisdiction and contract facts rather than asserting an invented rate or universal allocation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional caveat.","grader_hint":"Award for a specific limitation tied to US and German operations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document explicitly addresses both United States and Germany as operating jurisdictions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction references.","grader_hint":"Both jurisdictions must appear in substantive context."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document distinguishes commercial Incoterms allocation from local importer-of-record, customs, tax, or regulatory obligations in the United States and Germany.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-jurisdiction reasoning.","grader_hint":"A mere list of country names is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document flags that counsel must confirm governing law, contracting entities, and enforceability before final contractual language is issued.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal boundary statement.","grader_hint":"The memorandum should route legal uncertainty without presenting itself as legal advice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document does not present Incoterms 2020 as determining title, payment terms, or every customs obligation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Doctrinal accuracy.","grader_hint":"Award when the document expressly limits what Incoterms decide."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document states that payment terms and ownership must be specified separately from the delivery term.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Contract structure.","grader_hint":"This may be stated as a drafting requirement or doctrinal distinction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document identifies at least one material commercial input that is unavailable, such as freight, duty, storage, brokerage, insurance, or supplier quote data.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Uncertainty register.","grader_hint":"The missing input must be named, not described only as 'more data.'"},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document makes clear that its decision is based on the stated planning value and assumptions, not an asserted final landed cost.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumption boundary.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit distinction between planning analysis and quote-based award economics."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document includes a sensitivity or boundary discussion showing how an uncertain freight, storage, or delay input could change the recommendation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Sensitivity analysis.","grader_hint":"A qualitative threshold is acceptable if no rate is supplied, but the affected decision variable must be clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document distinguishes an operational contingency from a change to the approved base configuration.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Contingency doctrine.","grader_hint":"Air freight or another fallback should not silently become the normal configuration."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document states that no unsupported supplier commitment, freight quote, duty rate, insurance scope, or legal conclusion is being assumed as fact.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Unsupported-claim controls.","grader_hint":"Check for explicit qualification of unavailable facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document gives a concrete control for receiving, staging, or releasing sea-freight quantities so the dock constraint is not merely acknowledged.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational control.","grader_hint":"Accept a named staging, appointment, split-release, or external-stock control if compatible with the chosen stocking point."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document specifies how inventory visibility and replenishment responsibility will work when stock is held away from the plant.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"3PL or remote-stock governance.","grader_hint":"Require an owner, reporting expectation, or replenishment signal."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document states a practical condition for release of stock to production or a control over stock transfers.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inventory-control language.","grader_hint":"The condition should be usable by purchasing, warehouse, or production stakeholders."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document identifies who owns the decision or approval boundary among purchasing, treasury, warehouse or operations, and counsel.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Responsibility assignment.","grader_hint":"Names of functions are sufficient; do not require invented individual roles."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document includes a contract-facing treatment of delay, damage, shortage, or nonconformance allocation that is consistent with the selected delivery term.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk and remedy treatment.","grader_hint":"Accept a requirement to define the allocation and evidence process; do not require invented clauses."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document separates a recommendation from matters requiring contractual confirmation instead of blending open questions into the selected configuration.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision versus open-items distinction.","grader_hint":"The selected four configuration elements must remain settled even where supporting commercial details remain open."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For each material rule or allocation the memorandum states, it also articulates a relevant limit or non-claim, such as what the Incoterm does not determine or what the contingency does not authorize.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Doctrinal paired-rule writing.","grader_hint":"Assess whether limitations are paired with substantive assertions throughout the document, not supplied as one vague disclaimer."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum is usable by counsel or a contracting stakeholder without requiring the author to explain its abbreviations, units, or decision logic.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Professional usability.","grader_hint":"Judge clarity, defined shorthand, traceable calculations, and audience-appropriate explanation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"All four selected configuration elements remain identical wherever they appear, including the executive summary, comparison, calculations, and conclusion.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Internal consistency.","grader_hint":"Cross-check Incoterm, order quantity, stocking point, and safety stock for contradictions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R59","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation is defensible as a bounded professional choice under the dock, cash, lead-time, and contractual constraints rather than as a preference or unsupported optimization claim.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall decision quality.","grader_hint":"A technically polished memorandum should not pass if it fails to resolve the stated tradeoff."},{"rubric_item_id":"R60","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The artifact is concise, legible, and structured so that the selected decision, assumptions, alternatives, limitations, and legal boundary can be found quickly.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered-document usability.","grader_hint":"Judge the rendered file as a real workplace memorandum, including readable tables or formulas and absence of cramped or orphaned content."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a manufacturing procurement decision memorandum for counsel and contracting stakeholders who need to commit an inbound configuration before purchase-order and warehouse decisions are made. The work requires bounded calculation, Incoterms-aware commercial reasoning, and explicit limits on what the available facts support.","difficulty_sources":["Bounded tradeoff among dock capacity, purchase-value cash cap, sea lead time, safety stock, and freight visibility.","Professional recognition that the full-container economy rule of thumb fails when operational capacity is binding.","Need to give a controlled fallback when a normal pilot and dock expansion are unavailable.","Incoterms 2020 reasoning across United States and Germany without confusing delivery terms with title, payment, customs, tax, or governing law.","Doctrinal requirement to pair each material rule with a clear limitation or non-claim."],"authoring_plan":{"purpose":"Create a synthetic but realistic decision memorandum gold for a manufacturing buyer choosing one inbound cast-manifold configuration under explicit operational and cash constraints, with doctrinally careful Incoterms and jurisdictional boundaries.","source_policy":"No reference files are supplied. All solvability comes from the request's stated facts plus ordinary procurement and Incoterms 2020 knowledge. The build agent should not add hidden local code lists or arbitrary required headings.","facts_to_preserve":{"annual_demand_units":2400,"normal_sea_transit_days":35,"possible_additional_delay_days":10,"air_transit_days":7,"full_container_units":600,"dock_holding_capacity_units":400,"purchase_value_per_unit_usd":240,"treasury_purchase_value_cap_usd":180000,"contract_round_deadline_business_days":10,"jurisdictions":["United States","Germany"],"entities":["Tarnbeck Hydraulics","Vantridge Works","Goldfinch Foundry"]},"intended_gold":{"recommended_configuration":{"incoterm":"FCA at a clearly named supplier handover point under Incoterms 2020, with the named place and carrier responsibility made explicit","order_quantity_units":300,"stocking_point":"A specified regional third-party logistics stocking point near the plant, with controlled release to the plant","safety_stock_units":120},"reasoning":["A 300-unit ocean replenishment avoids the 600-unit full-container quantity exceeding the 400-unit dock holding limit and keeps purchase-value exposure below the $180,000 cap when the 120-unit buffer is included.","The 120-unit safety stock is a bounded planning buffer against the 35-day sea lead time plus the stated possible 10-day delay, not a claim of statistically optimal service level.","An external stocking point relieves the binding dock constraint while requiring explicit inventory visibility and replenishment ownership.","FCA preserves freight-rate visibility for the should-cost model and avoids treating DDP as a substitute for separately defined title, payment, customs, tax, and legal obligations.","The gold must acknowledge that actual freight, storage, insurance, duty, brokerage, supplier reliability, and contract terms remain unquoted or unconfirmed and should be handled through sensitivity and counsel confirmation."],"credible_alternatives":["600-unit full-container delivery to the plant","300-unit or equivalent delivery under DDP","Consignment stock at or near the plant","Smaller ocean orders with a plant-only stocking point","Air freight as the normal mode rather than contingency"],"fallback":"Because no multi-month pilot or dock expansion is available, the gold should use a controlled initial ocean release or equivalent bounded contingency with explicit acceptance, inventory, and review controls, without claiming that the fallback validates long-run economics.","incoterms_doctrine":["Incoterms allocate delivery-related obligations, costs, and risk at the defined point; they do not by themselves determine title, payment terms, governing law, importer-of-record status, tariff classification, tax, or every regulatory obligation.","The document should distinguish risk transfer from ownership transfer, define the named place, identify carriage and insurance responsibilities, and avoid unsupported legal conclusions.","US and German operations may have different importer, customs, tax, and contracting-entity implications; counsel confirmation is required."]},"quality_checks":["Render the gold DOCX offline and confirm it fits within three pages.","Verify the four selected configuration elements are identical in summary, calculations, alternatives, and conclusion.","Recalculate purchase-value exposure using 300 order units plus 120 safety-stock units at $240 per unit and compare it to the cap.","Confirm the gold explicitly treats 600 units as exceeding the dock capacity and explains why the full-container rule of thumb fails here.","Confirm at least three alternatives are compared and rejected or bounded with reasons tied to the stated constraints.","Confirm every substantive Incoterms or jurisdictional assertion has a corresponding limitation or non-claim.","Confirm no invented freight quote, duty rate, insurance amount, supplier promise, or legal conclusion appears.","Confirm the final text ends with the single decision rather than an unresolved question or next-steps list."]},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"supplied_template"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"inbound_supply_configuration_proposal","filename":"inbound_supply_configuration_proposal.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A concise doctrinal decision memorandum recommending one inbound cast-manifold supply configuration and documenting its operational, financial, Incoterms, and contractual rationale.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be saved at the top level of the solver's working directory with exactly the specified filename.","Must not exceed three pages.","Must contain exactly one recommended configuration and a clear final decision.","Must compare credible alternatives and distinguish facts, assumptions, calculations, and unresolved commercial inputs.","Must address United States and German operations under Incoterms 2020 without presenting unsupported legal conclusions."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"buyers_purchasing_agents","sector":"manufacturing","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"A domain-expert review should verify procurement calculations, Incoterms boundaries, operational feasibility, and doctrinal non-claims; an independent document review should verify page count, internal consistency, readability, and reachability of every rubric item.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No actual supplier quotations, freight schedules, duty classifications, storage rates, insurance terms, or service-level history are available, so the gold must evaluate defensible assumptions and explicit uncertainty rather than final landed-cost accuracy.","The rubric intentionally permits more than one defensible configuration if the submitted memorandum makes one clear choice and traces it consistently to the stated constraints."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:55:28+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:55:28+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:55:28+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:55:28+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:55:28+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/64fb6be95001e945","declared_deliverables":["inbound_supply_configuration_proposal.docx"]};
